shape theory.branching.coercion : theory.branching.canopy, theory.coupling.stability-ratio, theory.mixing.angle {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Section 12.6 (Coercion and Suppression).
//
// Definition 12.23 (Coercion): external asymmetric influence that
// skews a subsystem's distribution away from its structural canopy.
// Structural, not moral. Measurable: the difference between actual
// trace distribution and structural canopy.
//
// Theorem 12.24 (Asymmetry Is Coercion): any power asymmetry
// (rho(A,B) < 1) is inherently coercive.
//
// Theorem 12.25 (Coercion Produces Decoherence): suppressed branches
// represent unrealized relationships. Decoherence accumulates until
// a forcing shape collapses it.
//
// Theorem 12.28 (Coercion Distorts the Mixing Angle): effective theta
// shifts from structural theta. Suppressed branches: theta' < theta.
// Forced branches: theta' > theta.
//
// Definition 12.29 (Suppression): delta_s = p|cos(theta') - cos(theta)|.
// Measurable geometric difference between actual and free mixing angles.
//
// Derives from: theory.branching.canopy, theory.coupling.stability-ratio,
//               theory.mixing.angle
  """
}
